\section{RESEARCH PLAN}
\subsection{Motivation}

Traditionally, watching television is a passive activity:
All viewers receive the same TV content at the same
playback pace, regardless of individual preferences
and situations.
Not being able to select viewing pace or to interact
with content can be inconvenient and even unproductive 
sometimes. For instance, when one
is interrupted during watching a news program
(e.g., by a phone call or biological need), one may 
miss the information (e.g., stock quotes, weather forcast,
etc.) that he is waiting for.
Similarly, when
one misses a crucial point during viewing a distance
lecture, he may not be able to comprehend the materials
after that point.
It is desirable if {\em viewing interactivity}
---pause, fast-forward and instant replay---can
be supported so that one can time-shift a program at his 
own comfortable pace.

In addition to viewing interactivity, being able to 
interact with the content of a program (that I call
{\em content interactivity}) can enrich viewing experience.
Content interactivity is
especially beneficial to distance education. For instance,
kids can change the attributes (e.g., colors, size and position)
of the ``bunny'', ``big bird'' and
other characters in the {\it Sesame Street}.
A grade school student can learn American states
by interacting with a map.
A high school student can conduct science experiments
through a virtual lab. Many applications exist.

To support interactive TV, many studies suggest a server-based
architectures (e.g., \cite{ex80,ex49,ex54,ex1,ex86}),
in which a server schedules
its resources (i.e., memory and disk bandwidth)
to support viewing interactivity.
In a server-based architecture, the receivers are assumed
to be passive---simply receiving bits and rendering frames.
However, because of the typical long end-to-end transmission
delay between a server and a client and the Interet's limited bandwidth,
it is practically unfeasible for the server
to support ``real-time'' viewing interactivity for tens of
thousands of simultaneous users.  Furthermore,
on a broadcast channel (e.g., CNN), one simply cannot request
the server to pause or replay a program.
 
In this proposal, we present a client-based interactive DTV
architecture.


Recent advancements in data compression, networking
and image-based rendering technologies, and the
proliferation of infrastructures have created the the
resurgence of interest in the interactive television.
A full interactive system (e.g., telepresence) would allow
the person to be immersed in a distant environment
and act upon it as he was there, but we are far from
what is possible given the technology of the near future.
However, supporting viewing interactivity and
content interactivity (defined next) is within the
reach of the technology and economy in the next few years.

The degree of interactivity can be categories into
viewing, content and full interactivity:
\begin{jlist}
\item
Viewing interactivity: A viewer can select his own
spatio-temporal view on the TV content in real-time.
A spatial view can be a pan or a zoom. A temporal
view is created by a time-shift
VCR-like operation---pause, replay or fast-forward.
\item
Content interactivity: The viewer can change the content
of a program locally. For instance, the viewer can change
the attributes (e.g., colors, positions and speed)
of objects in scenes.
\item
Full interactivity: The viewer can convey a sense of
presence to a remote location.
\end{jlist}

I plan to investigate new hybrids of technologies for 
supporting viewing and content 
interactivity. 
My primary focus will be on the receiver 
technology (not on the head-end technology), specifically
on how to manage the receiver's system resources (i.e.,
memory and disk bandwidth), on how to
index and store multimedia data for efficient 
and effective search and retrieval, and on how to
build data and resource managers that can scale with
resource constraints and QoS requirements. In addition,
I will also investigate image content processing technology,
including image segmentation and rendering techniques
for enhancing interactivity. 

Although recent efforts have made progress in aforementioned
areas, many issues remain tantalizingly open.
I plan to address these open issues, and to build on the
work from several fields, most notably from the 
image processing, operating system and database fields.
I intend to combine these technologies in fruitful
ways to address the challenges of supporting
interactive TVs, which I believe will enrich applications
such as distance education, video surveillance and
consumer television, to name just a few.

\subsection{Enabling Viewing Interactivity}

Viewing interactivity allows a viewer to select a
spatio-temporal view of the scene.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Spatial view: A simple 
spatial view can be a pan or a zoom, which does not 
change the camera position. However, supporting
zoom out and pan requires frames to be larger than 
the display device. While potentially interesting,
pan and zoom do not seem to pose difficult
research questions, so I omit them here.
Instead, I propose exploring techniques to
construct panoramic views. For a single camera,
I will expolre the techniques to
knit temporal adjacent frames into a panoramic view.
For instance, a remote student can get a panoramic
view of the lecture hall, rather than just the
instructor, to feel the presence in the hall.
%
%For a single camera, this requires a method for locating 
%frames that contain smooth
%camera pans or tilts, from which the panoramic
%view can be synthesized. For multiple cameras 
%camera calibration and synchronization are 
%critical.

\item Time-shift operations: 
To enable viewing interactivity, we need a large cache
at the receiver. An economical way to extent the
receiver's cache is to add a disk to its DRAM.
Equipped with a large and
inexpensive disk, the receiver can cache a large amount of
media data. A viewer can pause a live 
program to take a break from viewing while the broadcast
stream continues arriving and being written to the local disk.
The viewer can resume watching the program after the pause
with a delay, or fast-forward the program to get back in synch with
the broadcast stream. 
One can also record programs at multiple channels at the same
time (which a tape-based VCR cannot do), filter out unwanted
content and produce a customized program.   
\end{enumerate}

Extending DRAM cache with a disk adds complexity
to the receiver's buffer management, especially that
video and audio data must be made available continuously
to meet playback deadlines, or playback interruptions
occur. In addition, to achieve acceptable interactive performance,
the buffer manager must make the requested data available
in main memory with minimum delay, e.g.,
when a viewer issues a replay,
response time should be very low.
Satisfying these performance requirements
with minimum DRAM (and hence at minimum cost) is a challenging task.

%Furthermore, the data description and organization plays a
%crucial role in the retrieval performance.
%The video/audio data must be indexed
%for supporting user-friendly replay and fast-forward.

\subsection{Enabling Content Interactivity}

A transmitted program must be in a digital format so that
the content of the scene can be interacted upon.
Fortunately, much low-hanging fruit 
in this area is ready to be plunked. 
For example, MPEG-4 has proposed standards to
separate interactive objects from the scenes, to change
visual or acoustic properties of an object, and to
specify the spatio-temporal relations between objects.

%However, what the standards do not specify 
