%robustness of matte extraction algorithms. 

Content interactivity can be enriched if the TV is connected
to a bi-directional Internet back-channel.
While a given broadcast stream remains the same
for all viewers, various data objects can be transmitted to
individual viewers via the Internet channel. 
%Data objects
%can be textual (e.g., an HTML page), binary (e.g., a java appelet),
%or continuous (e.g., a video/audio stream).
Image-based rendering techniques can then
overlay a number of data objects with the broadcast stream to
provide a customized program. 
Using distance learning as an example,
a student can download a 3D animated laboratory
during a science lecture to perform interactive experiments.
The instructor can quiz the students on-line to understand 
how the students absorb the lecture materials and make real-time
adjustments. Many other application exist.

If we do not need to store media data on disk, 
synchronize data objects to support content interactivity 
is straightforwad. However, since supporting viewing
interactivity may record data objects on disk and 
a viewer can record a TV program, we have to consider
how data should be stored on disk so that the latency
of retrieving these data for later plakback encounters
minimum overhead. In addition, some data objects may be
transient, e.g., a HTML page, and do not require storage.
Some data objects may not need to be stored since the
disk bandwidth cannot keep up with handling
so many objects.  If a data object
must be stored, the data object should
be stored adjacent to the video frames that are
rendered at the same time
(temporally correlated). I will investigate
efficient data organization and indexing schemes 
to improve the performance of content interactivity.
%temporal-spatio database

\subsection{QoS-Adaptive Content and Resource Management}

Since the available hardware resources differ from one
receiver to another and the QoS requirements at a
receiver can also differ from one time to another,
it is critical to design a system that is
adaptive to its environment. For example, the
processor in a receiver can be a low-end or a high-end
one (affecting decoding speed) and the amont of DRAM
in a set-top box can range widely. A viewer watching
a cooking lesson may care only seeing the recipe (texts)
at one time, but cares high video/audio quality 
when the dishes are cooked.

To summarize, the following technologies will be 
investigate for supporting viewing and content
interactivity:
\begin{jlist}
\item Viewing interactivity: video panoramas, real-time buffer 
management and media data indexing.
\item Content interactivity: image segmentation, media data 
indexing and organization.
\item QoS-Adaptive content and resource management:
QoS-adaptive resource scheduling.
\end{jlist}


